From 866af3b919e19776fa5145e0dde9e2113735c743 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:52:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/36] grate: port Tegra 2/3 GR3D driver to mainline Mesa 26.3

Brings libre-tegra's grate driver (Mesa 25.3.3, grate-wip) forward to
mainline and re-targets it at the legacy grate kernel UAPI, which is what
the postmarketOS grate kernel actually exposes (DRM version 99997.0.0).

Gallium 25.3.3 -> 26.3:
 - util_dynarray_append() is now 2-arg; use util_dynarray_append_typed()
 - pipe_context::clear() gained color/stencil clear masks
 - pipe_surface is a frontend-owned value type; drop grate_surface.c/h
   and the create_surface/surface_destroy hooks

Kernel UAPI: grate-wip targets the new CHANNEL_*/SYNCPOINT_* ioctls, which
this kernel does not implement (SYNCPOINT_ALLOCATE returns EINVAL, and
DRM_TEGRA_VERSION shares ioctl number 0x10 with mainline CHANNEL_OPEN, so
probing it appears to succeed with all-zero output). drm/{channel,job,
pushbuf,syncpt}.c are reimplemented on OPEN_CHANNEL/GET_SYNCPT/SUBMIT:
 - the syncpoint belongs to the channel, so syncpoint_new() takes one
 - the gather must live in a GEM object, so the pushbuf is staged into a BO
 - relocations name GEM handles and must stay in emit order, since the
   host1x firewall consumes one per address register it walks

BO fixes (pre-existing, in grate-wip):
 - grate_bo_alloc() never called grate_bo_prepare(), leaving refcnt at 0
 - grate_bo_free() left the sparse-array slot dirty, so a recycled GEM
   handle tripped the freshness assert; clear it, and keep the device
   pointer in grate_bo_unref() since the memset clears bo->drm

Verified on a Tegra 3 (Surface RT): GL 2.1/GLSL 1.20 and GLES 2.0 contexts,
gr2d clear executes on the GPU and reads back correctly, and weston's
gl-renderer initialises against the device. The fragment shader compiler
still implements only TGSI MOV, so draws produce no colour output.
