From f98803d9a57810b606ec44f47adcdfcb730eb45c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:03:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/36] grate/fp: implement immediates and the real ALU opcodes

The fragment compiler previously emitted only TGSI MOV, and treated every
non-INPUT source as a register index, so a constant-colour shader silently
read register 0 and drew nothing.

 - parse TGSI immediates and resolve them per component: 0.0 and 1.0 come
   free from the lowp constant register 31, anything else is allocated an
   fp20 embedded constant in the packet's fourth slot (registers 28..30)
 - add grate_fp20_from_float() and the ALU3 constant encoding. Those words
   are deliberately not byte-swapped, unlike regular ALU instructions
 - generalise emit_vMOV() into emit_alu(), expressing every op as the
   hardware's rA*rB + rC, and add MUL, ADD, MAD, MIN and MAX
 - pack the operand modifiers (negate/abs/scale/minus-one) and the result
   scale and condition code, which the IR carried but pack.c dropped
 - split into multiple ALU packets when constants steal the fourth slot
 - a shader with no varyings has no MFU instruction, and the barycentric
   setup was writing through the empty list head; give it one
 - reject sampler-view formats the hardware lacks in is_format_supported()
   instead of asserting later in resource_create()

Verified against softpipe with tests/fptest: 10/12 cases pass with a
maximum channel delta of 1, which is fp20/fx10 precision. Varyings and
texture sampling remain.
