From d1499f0268a33d7ea304d1178eebb967bb6d785d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:14:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/36] grate: fix multi-instruction fragment programs,
 varyings and VS immediates

Three separate defects, each of which silently produced black pixels.

Pixel sequencer starvation: REG_TGR3D_PSEQ_CTL was programmed with MAX_OUT
and MIN_OUT left at zero, so only the first EXEC of a fragment program ever
ran. Any shader the GLSL compiler split across two instructions lost
everything after the first. libgrate's grate_3d_set_alu_buffer_size() uses
0x12c/0xc8, so use those, and derive REG_TGR3D_GLOBAL_PIX_ATTR (libgrate's
ALU_BUFFER_SIZE) from the same figure rather than a magic 0x58000000.

Varying interpolation: the MFU var slot was keyed on the destination
component, but var[c] fetches TRAM component c into row register c, so it
has to be keyed on the source swizzle. A swizzled read fetched the wrong
component and could clobber an output register. The linker also marked all
four components fp20 regardless of use; grate's reference programs read
LINK fp20, fp20, NOP, NOP, tram0.xyzw, export1 - so honour the usage mask.

Vertex immediates: the compiler mapped TGSI immediates to uniform slot
1023 - index and never uploaded them, so they read as garbage. The constant
file is 256 vec4s, making those slots unreachable anyway. Record the values,
allocate them from the top of the real file, and upload them with the shader.

fptest now passes 14/15 against softpipe, varyings included, with a maximum
channel delta of 1 from fp20/fx10 precision. Only TEX remains.
