From 9661b6723940ecabbe1b92a37cb536dcdf8d25a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:47:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/36] grate/tests: cover the texture stride cases weston
 exposes

Adds the cases found while bringing weston up, including the ones that still
fail, so the gap is recorded rather than rediscovered.

tex_500 uses a 500x400 texture, the size of weston-image's toplevel. It
shears, and measuring the drift pins the cause down: 32 bytes per row, which
is the difference between the pitch resource_create() allocates,
align(500*4, 32) = 2016, and next_pot(500)*4 = 2048. The sampler derives the
row stride from the power-of-two extent enclosing the width, and the texture
descriptor has no stride field to correct it with.

Allocating sampled surfaces at that stride does fix tex_500, but scanout and
imported buffers have to keep the stride the display controller and the
importer agreed on, so it cannot be applied to them - and weston's client
buffers arrive that way. Fixing this properly means staging an imported
buffer through a correctly laid out texture, so the speculative allocation
change is left out and only the tests land.

tex_npot covers the other half: the last rows of a 5x3 texture sample as
zero, the vertical analogue of the same power-of-two behaviour.
