From 41a87c7b67514ee99695253cc9f70aba28da7e6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:56:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/36] grate: allocate sampled surfaces at the stride the
 sampler assumes

A texture descriptor has no stride field. The sampler derives the row stride
from the power-of-two extent enclosing the width, not from the width, so a
texture allocated at align(width*bpp, 32) is read with a growing per-row
offset and the image skews.

Measured on a 500x400 texture, the size of weston-image's toplevel: the drift
is 32 bytes per row, exactly next_pot(500)*4 - align(500*4, 32) = 2048 - 2016.
It also explains why 250x250 looked fine, since align(1000,32) and
next_pot(250)*4 are both 1024, and why a 5x3 texture works horizontally,
since next_pot(5)*4 and align(20,32) are both 32.

So allocate sampled surfaces at next_pot(width) * blocksize. Buffers anyone
outside the driver can see - scanout, display targets, anything shared or
required to be linear - keep the stride the display controller and importers
agreed on, because changing that corrupts scanout; they sample incorrectly
instead. Imported client buffers still land in that category, and fixing them
means staging through a correctly laid out texture.

tests/fptest: 85/88, tex_500 now exact. The remaining three are the last rows
of a 5x3 texture, the vertical analogue of the same behaviour.

Also adds tests/weston-grate and tests/weston-grate-shot: run weston on the
panel through the drm backend, and capture its screen.
