From 04c4500f134c46ca72dbd00e89d18fe3d3283537 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 16:13:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/36] grate: do not flip sampled textures, and add a
 render-target size test

The previous commit set DRM_TEGRA_GEM_CREATE_BOTTOM_UP when allocating
sampled surfaces at the power-of-two stride. That flag is for render targets,
whose origin is bottom left; on a texture uploaded top down it flips the
image, which is what showed on the panel.

tests/rttest renders a gradient into a framebuffer object of a given size and
reads it back, which the fragment tests could not catch because they only ever
drew into a 64x64 - power-of-two - pbuffer.

It documents a pre-existing limitation, present before the stride work and
unchanged by disabling it: a render target only rasterises correctly up to a
certain size. Width alone is fine, 1366x64 is exact, but 1024x768 draws only
the top band and 1366x768 produces nothing. There are no kernel messages, no
GL errors, and raising the pixel sequencer's MAX_OUT/MIN_OUT to the field
maximum changes nothing, so the limit is somewhere else.

This is what stops weston being usable on the panel at 1366x768: the
compositor renders into an output-sized target, so most of the frame is never
drawn. Everything else - the fragment pipeline, sampling, the gl-renderer
starting - works at smaller sizes.

    ./rttest 1366 768        # sample five points
    ./rttest 1366 768 scan   # report the last row that rendered
