From cd8707b3a2c94a63008ba8d9246ef66d1a1ddd2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:11:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/36] grate: wait for the GPU, and fix texture stride,
 scanout flip and faults

Five defects that together made a compositor unusable, found by bringing
weston up on the panel at 1366x768.

Nothing ever waited for the GPU. Draws submit with wait=false and free the
job immediately, pipe_context::flush was an empty stub, flush_resource was an
empty stub, and transfer_map handed out a CPU pointer without synchronising.
A readback therefore raced the GPU still drawing, which looked exactly like a
size limit: small targets happened to finish in time, 1366x768 never did.
Sleeping 200ms before reading made it correct, which is what gave it away.
Streams now remember the fence of their last submission - the job is gone by
the time anyone asks to wait - and flush, flush_resource and transfer_map all
wait on it. flush_resource matters most for weston: it is called as a buffer
is handed to the window system, so without it the compositor scans out a
frame that is still being drawn.

Texture stride. A texture descriptor carries no stride field. A power-of-two
texture is described by LOG2_WIDTH/LOG2_HEIGHT and keeps its natural pitch;
any other size is described by WIDTH/HEIGHT and the sampler assumes the width
rounded up to 64 bytes. Allocating anything else skews the image. The
alignment was found by sweeping it against tests/fptest, which only passes in
full at 64. Scanout buffers use the same rule, since the pitch chosen here is
what resource_get_handle() hands to KMS.

SMMU page faults. The sampler addresses rows inside the power-of-two extent
enclosing the height, so a non-power-of-two texture has to have those rows
backed or tegra-mc reports texsrd2 page faults. A scanned out buffer is the
exception: BOTTOM_UP puts the image at the end of the allocation, so padding
it would push the visible rows off the top.

Scanout orientation. Mesa flips a window system framebuffer by handing over a
negative Y scale, assuming GL's bottom left origin. This hardware rasterises
top down, so for a buffer being scanned out the flip has to come back off or
the display shows the frame upside down. Offscreen targets keep it: they are
read back through GL, where the flip is what makes them come out right.

BOTTOM_UP is no longer set on sampled textures, which are uploaded top down
and were coming out upside down.

tests/: rttest renders a gradient into a framebuffer object of any size,
orient checks which way up a window system framebuffer lands, and
install-mesa.sh installs over the system copy - ninja install only writes to
the meson prefix, and a stale /usr/lib copy silently tests old code.

tests/fptest: 142/142. Render targets are correct at every size tried, up to
1366x768. weston's gl-renderer composites a full desktop correctly offscreen
at panel resolution.
