From 449234066a5c1286b46c1f40b56f246e7ca30b1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:18:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/36] grate: get the scanned out framebuffer the right way up
 and the right size

Follows the synchronisation and stride work with the scanout specific parts,
found by running weston on the panel at 1366x768.

Orientation: mesa flips a window system framebuffer by handing over a
negative Y scale, assuming GL's bottom left origin. This hardware rasterises
top down, so a buffer that is scanned out needs that flip taken back off, or
the display shows the frame upside down. Offscreen targets keep it, since
they are read back through GL where the flip is what makes them come out the
right way up. Confirmed by tracing both: the drm backend asks for -384 where
the headless one asks for +384, and the headless output was already correct.

Allocation: a scanned out buffer keeps exactly the rows it was told about.
Padding it to the power-of-two extent, which sampled textures need so the
sampler does not read past the end, pushes the visible rows out of place.
BOTTOM_UP likewise now only applies to a target nobody samples: on a texture
it stands the image on its head, and on the scanout buffer the viewport
already handles the flip.

With this the compositor draws a correct desktop on the panel: background,
window decorations, terminal text and colours are all crisp and upright.
