From 8b731a0cad23e2d3ff13e0ea6988352cfd47a3c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:42:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/36] grate: add SEQ, SNE and LRP, and the groundwork for the
 SFU ops

Xwayland's glamor shaders wanted FLR, SEQ, FRC, RCP and SNE, and every one of
them fell through to "UNIMPLEMENTED", which is why X content came out as
garbage while weston's own shaders - which use none of them - were fine.

SEQ is a condition code the ALU already has. SNE has none, so it is SEQ turned
inside out with a new 1-s0 source form. LRP is s2 + s0*(s1-s2), which needs the
difference worked out first. Both borrow a scratch vec4 from just past the
temporaries the shader declared, so they go through the ordinary register
allocator and cost nothing in shaders that do not use them.

RCP, FRC, RSQ, SQRT and FLR are written but switched off unless GRATE_FP_SFU is
set. The special function unit itself is fine: sweeping the 4-bit opcode field
against a known input reproduces RCP, RSQ, LG2, SQRT and FRC to within a bit of
what softpipe computes, confirming the opcode table. A shader consisting of
nothing but one of them gives exactly the right answer. What does not work yet
is reading the answer back when the shader does anything else too - as soon as
an ordinary ALU instruction shares the program the MFU result reads as zero -
so this is a scheduling hazard between the MFU and ALU stages rather than the
arithmetic. Wrong pixels are worse than an honest "unimplemented", so it waits.

The suite grows from 142 to 214 cases. The new ones include plain regression
cases for chaining a value through a temporary and for writing one output
component per instruction, neither of which anything covered before. The
special function cases are named sfu_* and are skipped unless GRATE_FP_SFU is
set, so the suite still reports what is actually true.

GRATE_FP_TRACE now dumps the incoming TGSI, each opcode as it is translated,
and the scheduled MFU/ALU program, which is how the above was worked out.
