From c7e5da13627a4c84a793fe56d7439a8f3dc7e732 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 10:32:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 24/36] grate: stage extra vertex fetches, split long draws,
 and give depth its own surface

Three bugs, all of which were asserts that the packaged build compiles away
with b_ndebug, so each one silently produced wrong geometry instead of failing.

The vertex unit fetches at most one attribute and one uniform per instruction.
Anything naming two different uniforms - which is every matrix times vector -
was packed with the wrong index, so a uniform transform put geometry wherever
it liked. Stage the extras through temporaries first. This is the big one:
glxgears was drawing hugely over-scaled geometry, which is why it managed only
28fps; it now runs at 62 and is vsync limited, with the gears the right size
and the right colours. It also un-wedged the compositor, which used to stop
servicing screencopy after an EGL client ran.

IDX_DRAW_PRIM's vertex count is a 12 bit field, so a draw longer than 4096
vertices wrapped and a mesh of any size came out as a handful of stray
triangles. Split on primitive boundaries, repeating the shared vertices for
strips and keeping a triangle strip's advance even so the winding does not
flip. Fans and loops need the first vertex or the closing edge in every chunk
and are clamped with a message instead. glmark2's horse now draws whole.

QR_Z_TEST's Z_SURF_PTR was never set, so it named surface 0 - the colour
buffer - and the depth unit read and wrote depth over the colours. The depth
buffer was also listed in DW_ST_ENABLE, which had the fragment stage store
colour into it. Point the depth unit at the surface the depth buffer actually
occupies and keep it out of the colour store mask.

Depth is still not right: with depth writes enabled part of the colour buffer
comes out wrong, and it is not the depth surface's stride, orientation or
address, all of which are now confirmed correct. tests/bigdraw reproduces it
in isolation against softpipe - run with USE_DEPTH=1 - and shows that the test
and the surface pointer are fine, since ALWAYS with depth writes masked off is
pixel exact. Left for the next pass.

New tests: vstest (uniform matrix transform), persptest (perspective correct
interpolation, which turned out to be exact), bigdraw (draws past the packet
limit, plus the depth reproduction), gradtest (per-pixel varying gradient).
