From f7adf4f2584e3646b9db5b0a1131790af019259b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 11:10:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/36] grate: record what the hardware offers for blending,
 and keep the blend state

There is no blending in the register set. The data write stage has a logic op
and nothing else, and no register anywhere carries a blend factor, an equation
or a blend colour.

The hardware does have one destination read path: GLOBAL_MEMORY_OUTPUT_READS'
READ_DEST bit, which the driver was programming to zero. Turning it on works in
the sense that the destination pixel comes back - the blend test returns the
blue background instead of the red source - but the value lands in R2-R3 after
the fragment program has run, replacing whatever it put there, and sweeping
DW_LOGIC_OP through all sixteen values changes nothing. So the destination is
readable by the data write stage and not by the shader, which is what combining
source and destination would need. Reading it back also hangs gr3d, so it stays
off, and the finding is written down where the register is programmed.

bind_blend_state now keeps the state instead of calling grate_unimplemented(),
so the plumbing is in place; it still changes nothing about what is drawn.
Everything renders opaque. tests/blendtest measures it against softpipe: 50%
red over blue should be 128,0,128 and comes out 255,0,0.

Doing this properly needs the render target bound as a texture and the shader
sampling it at gl_FragCoord, which the fragment compiler cannot express yet.
