From 594ce0071402649c9beab9d42398d79d5462323d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 12:46:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/36] grate: give mip levels storage, and stop sampling
 levels that are not there

Nothing looked at last_level. An object was sized for level 0 only, so a
texture with a mip chain had the sampler reading past the end of it, which
faults the SMMU and hangs gr3d hard enough to take the machine down: glmark2's
texture scene rebooted this device twice. Uploads were as bad, since
transfer_map ignored the level and wrote every one of them over level 0, and
rendering into a level did the same because the surface relocation used offset
zero regardless.

So: lay the levels out one after another, hand transfer_map the level's offset
and pitch, and relocate a render target to the level it names. The texture
scene now runs to completion at 62fps with no hang.

The sampler still only reads level 0, now by saying so with BASE_LEVEL_ONLY
rather than by accident. With LOD_MIN/LOD_MAX set and LERP_MIP on, a texture
whose levels are flat distinct colours still reads level 0 at every
minification where softpipe picks level 2 and level 4 (tests/miptest), so the
descriptor's per-level addressing is not understood yet. Minification aliases
as a result, which is what makes glmark2's crate look noisy.

Also: NORMALIZE is normalized texture coordinates, which GL always wants. It
was being used as a mipmap disable, so asking for a mipmapped texture quietly
cost normalized coordinates as well.

Sampling itself is correct, which took some proving because every texture case
in the suite used NEAREST and CLAMP_TO_EDGE - exactly what a null sampler state
falls back to - so none of them would have noticed a filter that never arrived.
tests/mintest checks nearest and linear, magnified and minified, against
softpipe with high frequency content, and matches.
