From aca0937d35b722a2150a55642c9d89e2f80ed6ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 13:26:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 28/36] grate: translate vertex shaders from NIR instead of
 going through TGSI

Mesa hands the driver NIR and it was being thrown straight back out through
nir_to_tgsi so the old TGSI translator could pick it up again. Consume the NIR.

grate_nir_lower_vs flattens the shader, lowers io to load_input/store_output
and optimises until it stops making progress; uniforms deliberately stay as
load_uniform, since the vertex unit has one flat constant file and no notion of
a buffer to load from. vp/nir.c then walks the result. There is no register
allocator: every SSA definition gets a temporary of its own, which is honest
for a file of 62 and a unit with no control flow, and running out is reported
rather than papered over.

Two things the hardware insists on, both of which are silent when wrong:

  - Operands sit in fixed slots and ADD is the odd one out, reading src0 and
    src2 and never looking at src1. Filling the slots in source order computes
    with a slot the instruction does not read, and the geometry simply goes
    somewhere else - which is exactly what the first working version did.
  - At most one attribute and one uniform may be fetched per instruction, so
    the extras are staged through temporaries here too, the same as the TGSI
    path had to.

vecN becomes one masked move per component, since each component can come from
somewhere different.

tests/fptest passes 214/214 at tolerance 2 with the vertex path on NIR, and
vstest, persptest, rttest, orient, mintest, bigdraw and gbmtest all agree with
softpipe. glmark2-es2-drm straight on KMS scores 165 with no GPU faults.

GRATE_VS_TGSI=1 forces the old path for comparison. The fragment shader still
goes through nir_to_tgsi; that is the next one.
