From d5da652f0b8ac0aa26d25fa6fc790144eaf457fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 13:44:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/36] grate: start the fragment NIR translator, and keep both
 stages opt-in

fp/nir.c translates fragment shaders straight from NIR. The fragment ALU is
scalar with four slots to a packet, so NIR is scalarised and every SSA
definition gets one scalar register, which suits a file of 19 far better than
the vec4 granularity the TGSI path had to work at.

It is not finished: 187 of the suite's 214 cases pass, and lrp, tex_mul and
two_out_same are still wrong. So both translators are opt-in - GRATE_VS_NIR
and GRATE_FS_NIR - and everything on the device keeps running through TGSI,
which passes 214/214. Turning the fragment side on by default while it was
still short is what made weston look wrong, and that was the wrong way round:
a half finished translator has no business being the default.

Things the NIR path has to do that TGSI did not:

  - NIR gives feq/fneu plus b2f32 where TGSI gave seq/sne, so bools are
    lowered to floats first; this hardware has no booleans.
  - vecN only gathers, so resolving a component has to look through it, and so
    does the check for whether a source is a varying and needs the MFU.
  - The colour the ALU builds up accumulates in R2-R3 across instructions, so
    the store belongs on the last instruction rather than on every write.
  - Embedded constants ride in a packet's fourth slot, so only three writes fit
    beside them and the output has to be split across packets.
  - sne has no condition code of its own and is computed as an equality turned
    inside out through a scratch register.

The shared tail - refusing to hand the GPU an untranslatable program, and the
barycentric setup - is now grate_fp_finish() rather than a copy in each
translator, and the linker word macros moved to fpir.h.

Also reverted lower_flrp32/lower_fmod from the compiler options: they changed
the NIR that nir_to_tgsi sees and broke lrp on the TGSI path, which is a real
regression for something that was only meant to help the new one.
