From 94356476832b625b55e2f7897db32710a67cc76a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 13:48:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 30/36] grate: keep a copy of each varying the fragment MFU
 interpolates

Asking the MFU for the same varying twice does not give the same answer the
second time, so a component read in two instructions came back as two
different values - and both wrong. tests/fptest's new vmul2 case is the
smallest shape that shows it: uv.x * 0.5 and uv.x * 0.25 in one shader, where
uv.x reads as 0.102 in one instruction and something else in the other, when it
is 0.133.

So the first instruction that needs a varying interpolates it as before and
also keeps a copy in a spare slot of the same packet, and later instructions
read that copy. Keeping the interpolation attached to the instruction that
consumes it matters: hoisting it into an instruction of its own, which was the
obvious way to write this, gives the wrong value even for a single read.

That fixes reading one varying once, reading two components of one varying,
and a plain varying passthrough. Reading the same component from two
instructions is still wrong when the first use is a store_output, because that
path packs its own slots and does not take part in keeping copies yet.

The fragment path is 196 of 241 and stays opt-in behind GRATE_FS_NIR.
Everything on the device runs TGSI, which passes 241/241, as does the vertex
NIR path.

New cases: vmul1, vmul2 and vmulxy, which pin down varying reuse specifically.
