From cfafd44b7f7cd4fa43fef956b59ba19d4c1e2818 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Pavlovic <sam@sampavlovic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 14:07:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/36] grate: complete the NIR fragment translator

The fragment path now passes the same 241 cases the TGSI one does.

Four things were wrong. Instructions were flushed after an operation's
sources had been resolved, which stranded an embedded constant in the
packet just emitted while the operation reading it landed in the next
one; the decision is now taken before any source is resolved. The batch
limit was three operations, but the ALU issues up to three packets per
instruction, so nine always fit. fneg and fabs were each given an
instruction of their own when every source operand already carries the
modifiers. And an output write went to a temporary that a later
instruction copied out, which is not merely wasteful: a varying's
interpolated value depends on how many instructions the program has, and
a value derived from a varying does not survive the round trip. The
operation that produces a component is now retargeted straight at the
output register, for every component before any copy is emitted, so that
needing a copy for one component does not cost the others their fusion.

Also lower flrp, which the ALU has no instruction for, and add
GRATE_NIR_DUMP to print the shader the translators are handed.
